My prediction is that we will keep building, and with the transformer archi=
tecture for at least a couple more years, at least when it comes to state-o=
f-the-art modeling performance.</p><p>At the same time, I do think that we =
will see more and more of these efficiency and engineering tweaks like Gate=
d DeltaNet and Mamba layers because at the scale at which LLMs are trained,=
 deployed, and used, it just makes sense from a financial perspective for t=
hese companies, which are still burning a lot of money on serving LLMs.</p>=
<p><span>This doesn=E2=80=99t mean that there are no other alternatives out=
 there. As I=E2=80=99ve written about in </span><a href=3D"https://magazine=
.sebastianraschka.com/p/beyond-standard-llms">Beyond Standard LLMs</a><span=
>, for instance, text diffusion models are an interesting approach. Right n=
ow, they fall into the category of experimental research models, but </span=
><a href=3D"https://deepmind.google/models/gemini-diffusion">Google shared<=
/a><span> that they will release a Gemini Diffusion model. It won=E2=80=99t=
 rival their state-of-the-art offerings in modeling quality, but it will be=
 really fast and attractive for tasks with low-latency requirements (e.g., =
code completion).</span></p><p><span>Also, two weeks ago, the open-weight <=
/span><a href=3D"https://github.com/inclusionAI/LLaDA2.0">LLaDA 2.0 models =
dropped</a><span>. The largest one, at 100B parameters, is the largest text=
 diffusion model to date and is on par with Qwen3 30B. (Yes, it doesn=E2=80=
=99t push the state-of-the-art overall, but it=E2=80=99s still a notable re=
lease in the diffusion model space.)</span></p><h1 class=3D"header-anchor-p=
ost">4. It=E2=80=99s Also The Year of Inference-Scaling and Tool Use<div cl=
