th d=3D"M14 11a5 5 0 0 0-7.54-.54l-3 3a5 5 0 0 0 7.07 7.07l1.71-1.71"></pat=
h></svg></button></div></div></h1><p>If I had to pick a word or trend that =
describes LLM development this year, it would be =E2=80=9Cbenchmaxxing=E2=
=80=9D.</p><p>=E2=80=8BHere, benchmaxxing means there=E2=80=99s a strong fo=
cus on pushing leaderboard numbers, sometimes to the point where benchmark =
performance becomes a goal in itself rather than a proxy for general capabi=
lity.</p><p>A prominent example was Llama 4, which scored extremely well ac=
ross many established benchmarks. However, once users and developers got th=
eir hands on it, they realized that these scores didn=E2=80=99t reflect the=
 real-world capabilities and usefulness.</p><p>As the popular saying goes, =
if the test set is public, it isn=E2=80=99t a real test set. And the proble=
m these days is that test set data is not only part of the training corpus =
(intentionally or unintentionally), but is also often directly optimized fo=
r during LLM development.</p><p><span>=E2=80=8BBack in the day, even if ben=
chmark scores on public test sets were inflated, at least the model ranking=
 was still preserved. E.g., see the annotated figure from the 2019 </span><=
a href=3D"https://arxiv.org/abs/1902.10811">Do ImageNet Classifiers General=
ize to ImageNet</a><span>? paper below.</span></p><div class=3D"captioned-i=
